WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.229 --> 00:00:04.230
With the core concepts of networking within Unreal now covered,

2
00:00:04.230 --> 00:00:07.730
it's time we look at how our data gets from the server to the client,

3
00:00:07.730 --> 00:00:11.310
namely the actor replication flow.

4
00:00:11.310 --> 00:00:13.310
In terms of the replication flow,

5
00:00:13.310 --> 00:00:18.309
the bulk of the work takes place in the server replicate actors function of the net driver.

6
00:00:18.309 --> 00:00:23.660
This is the function where the server determines what is relevant to each client.

7
00:00:23.660 --> 00:00:26.160
Once all actors have been gathered for each client,

8
00:00:26.160 --> 00:00:28.660
the server will then replicate components and their data,

9
00:00:28.660 --> 00:00:33.159
as well as data within the actors directly that has changed since the last update.

10
00:00:33.159 --> 00:00:37.880
When it comes to replication, the actor class provides key replication framework functions

11
00:00:37.880 --> 00:00:42.380
and variables that control how and when an actor is replicated across the network.

12
00:00:42.380 --> 00:00:46.200
The functions and properties listed here are the key ones that we really should take note of,

13
00:00:46.200 --> 00:00:49.909
so let's cover each one and see what they do.

14
00:00:49.909 --> 00:00:52.909
Firstly we've got the net update frequency which we covered earlier.

15
00:00:52.909 --> 00:00:55.909
This determines how often an actor replicates.

16
00:00:55.909 --> 00:00:59.570
Next we've got the pre-replication function inside of actor,

17
00:00:59.570 --> 00:01:03.070
and this is called before any replication actually takes place.

18
00:01:03.070 --> 00:01:06.159
From there we've got the be only relevant to owner bool,

19
00:01:06.159 --> 00:01:10.159
and setting this to true means we only replicate down to our owning client.

20
00:01:10.159 --> 00:01:15.189
Next we've got the is relevancy owner for function inside the actor.

21
00:01:15.189 --> 00:01:17.689
So if we are only relevant to our owning client,

22
00:01:17.689 --> 00:01:20.689
we fall into the is relevancy owner for function

23
00:01:20.689 --> 00:01:24.189
to bind onto any custom relevancy logic we may have set up.

24
00:01:24.189 --> 00:01:28.439
Lastly we've got the is net relevant for function inside of actor.

25
00:01:28.439 --> 00:01:30.439
So if we're not only relevant to our owner,

26
00:01:30.439 --> 00:01:33.939
we're going to fall into this backup is net relevant for function

27
00:01:33.939 --> 00:01:35.939
to see if there's any custom logic here.

28
00:01:35.939 --> 00:01:39.129
With those functions and properties in mind,

29
00:01:39.129 --> 00:01:43.129
we can now take a look at the high level flow of the replication process.

30
00:01:43.129 --> 00:01:47.450
Firstly we loop over all actively replicated actors.

31
00:01:47.450 --> 00:01:51.450
By this we mean we refer to any actors that have the be replicates flag set to true.

32
00:01:51.450 --> 00:01:56.090
From there we determine if this actor is initially dormant.

33
00:01:56.090 --> 00:01:59.540
If it comes back as dormant, we can just ignore it.

34
00:01:59.540 --> 00:02:04.040
Next we determine if this actor needs to update by checking the net update frequency value.

35
00:02:04.040 --> 00:02:08.780
So if it replicated too recently, we can ignore this one as well.

36
00:02:08.780 --> 00:02:13.280
Next we check if the actor is only relevant to the owner falling into the previously mentioned

37
00:02:13.280 --> 00:02:16.330
is relevancy owner for function.

38
00:02:16.330 --> 00:02:20.830
If this returns true, then we add this actor to the list of actors owned by the connection.

39
00:02:20.830 --> 00:02:24.419
If it returns false, we just ignore it.

40
00:02:24.419 --> 00:02:26.419
Next we call the pre replication function,

41
00:02:26.419 --> 00:02:31.919
and this function allows developers to decide if properties within the actor want to replicate to that given connection.

42
00:02:32.990 --> 00:02:35.490
It's worth noting that if you want to utilize this functionality,

43
00:02:35.490 --> 00:02:42.229
you must use the do rep lifetime active override macro in your get lifetime replicated props function.

44
00:02:42.229 --> 00:02:44.229
If this actor passes all of these tests,

45
00:02:44.229 --> 00:02:49.560
we're going to add it to a list of actors that we're going to consider for this particular update.

46
00:02:49.560 --> 00:02:52.560
So now that we've built a list of actors that we want to replicate,

47
00:02:52.560 --> 00:02:55.060
we need to see if we can replicate them.

48
00:02:55.060 --> 00:02:57.879
Firstly, we determine if the actor is dormant.

49
00:02:57.879 --> 00:03:01.379
If it's not, we then see if it has an actor channel open yet.

50
00:03:01.379 --> 00:03:06.039
The actor channel essentially being a connection between the server and the client representation.

51
00:03:06.039 --> 00:03:08.039
If the actor channel is not open yet,

52
00:03:08.039 --> 00:03:12.539
we need to check if it's because the client has yet to load into the level that the actor resides in.

53
00:03:12.539 --> 00:03:16.840
If the client hasn't loaded that level, we're just going to skip this actor.

54
00:03:16.840 --> 00:03:22.340
Next, we determine if the actor is relevant to this connection by calling the is net relevant for function.

55
00:03:22.340 --> 00:03:26.199
If it's not relevant, then this actor will be skipped.

56
00:03:26.199 --> 00:03:32.819
If we pass all of these checks, we can assume this actor is now owned by this connection and can be free to replicate it.

57
00:03:32.819 --> 00:03:38.020
With our list of actors that are relevant to this connection, we perform the following steps.

58
00:03:38.020 --> 00:03:42.520
First, we sort the actor by priority, and then for each of those sorted actors,

59
00:03:42.520 --> 00:03:46.520
we check that the connection hasn't loaded the level that this actor is in yet.

60
00:03:46.520 --> 00:03:50.159
If there's an actor channel open, we close it.

61
00:03:50.159 --> 00:03:56.659
Every second, we determine if this actor is relevant for this connection by calling its is net relevant for function.

62
00:03:56.659 --> 00:04:00.939
If it's not relevant for five seconds, then we close the channel entirely.

63
00:04:00.939 --> 00:04:05.439
If the actor is relevant and we don't have an actor channel for it, we'll open one.

64
00:04:05.439 --> 00:04:11.710
When dealing with a saturated connection, we fall into a deferred update cycle of sorts.

65
00:04:11.710 --> 00:04:17.500
We loop through the remaining actors in the list and assess how long they've been relevant or irrelevant for.

66
00:04:17.500 --> 00:04:22.500
If they've been relevant for less than a second, we forcibly push an update into the next tick.

67
00:04:22.500 --> 00:04:26.319
We can't possibly have drifted too far out of sync from the server in that time.

68
00:04:26.319 --> 00:04:29.819
If they've been relevant for more than a second, we call the is net relevant for

69
00:04:29.819 --> 00:04:34.079
to determine if we do need to push an update into the next tick at all.

70
00:04:34.079 --> 00:04:38.579
For any actors that pass all of these checks, the actor is replicated to the connection

71
00:04:38.579 --> 00:04:42.269
using the U channel replicate actor function.

72
00:04:42.269 --> 00:04:45.769
We started in the server replicate actors function in the net driver,

73
00:04:45.769 --> 00:04:50.269
and we've now covered how we decide what actors will be replicated to a connection.

74
00:04:50.269 --> 00:04:54.750
It's time now that we look at how we replicate actors through that connection.

75
00:04:54.750 --> 00:04:58.279
We start with the replicate actor function inside U channel.

76
00:04:58.279 --> 00:05:03.279
This function is responsible for replicating an actor and all of its components to that connection.

77
00:05:03.279 --> 00:05:06.819
With this, there is also a flow that we can take a look at, but don't worry,

78
00:05:06.819 --> 00:05:10.529
it is much more simple than the previous ones.

79
00:05:10.529 --> 00:05:14.529
Firstly, we determine if this is the first update since this actor channel was opened,

80
00:05:14.529 --> 00:05:19.220
and if so, we serialize any specific information that is needed.

81
00:05:19.220 --> 00:05:22.720
Next, we determine if the connection owns the actor in question.

82
00:05:22.720 --> 00:05:25.720
If it's not owned and the actor's role is autonomous proxy,

83
00:05:25.720 --> 00:05:30.439
then the role will be downgraded to simulated proxy.

84
00:05:30.439 --> 00:05:33.439
Next, we replicate any changed properties within the actor,

85
00:05:33.439 --> 00:05:37.759
and then we replicate any changed properties within the actor's components.

86
00:05:37.759 --> 00:05:41.759
Finally, for any deleted components, we're going to send a special delete command.
